ch should be one content character plus padding. The open question is what happens in the last-resort reduction when the fixed columns already overflow, and I want to protect the flexible columns there so they keep their minimum.\n\nI'm checking how the flexible-column minimum interacts with the fixed-column widths and the ratio distribution, since the negative remaining width could break the sum of ratios. I want to confirm whether a flexible column with a ratio of zero can slip through the filter and make the total zero, so I'll walk through that case and then test it.\n\nI'm checking the ratio distribution next: if every width is zero, the padding step would hit its assertion, and a negative leftover width would clamp each column to zero. I also need to confirm the empty-table case, since an empty column list would fail the same assertion.\n\nI'm looking at the ratio distribution now: if the total ratio is zero or less, the code needs a guard so it doesn't run into the assertion, and I want to confirm how the edge cases of an empty table or a negative remaining width are handled.\n\nI'm checking how `ratio_distribute` behaves when its ratios total zero or go negative, since that would hit the assertion or produce negative widths. For an all-zero total I'd fall back to equal ratios, and an empty list should simply return nothing. The open question is whether to clamp a negative total to zero before distributing.\n\nI'm making `ratio_distribute` safe for an empty list and for a total that is zero or negative, so it doesn't trip its assertion or hand out negative widths. Ratios that total zero would fall back to equal shares, while the minimums still hold.\n\nI'm making ratio_distribute handle the cases that would currently trip its assertion: an empty list should just return nothing, and if the ratios add up to zero or less, I'll fall back to equal shares so the padding step doesn't break.\n\nI'm now working on how the last-resort reduction treats flexible columns: